# Rule: SwiftUI View Hierarchy & Composition

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/specifying-the-view-hierarchy-of-an-app-using-a-scene
https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/layering-content
https://developer.apple.com/tutorials/swiftui-concepts/choosing-the-right-way-to-hide-a-view
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/view-fundamentals
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/view-configuration
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/view-groupings

Scope

Applies to how SwiftUI views are structured, composed, grouped, layered,
configured, and conditionally included in a hierarchy.

This rule governs:

- root view composition
- subview extraction
- container choice
- modifier placement
- overlay/background usage
- conditional inclusion and hiding

Intent

Build SwiftUI interfaces as clear, stable hierarchies of small views.

Compose UI from focused subviews and purpose-driven containers.
Use modifiers to configure views in place.
Use layering APIs intentionally.
Choose conditional removal vs visual hiding deliberately.

The resulting hierarchy must be:

- readable
- modular
- semantically clear
- easy to evolve
- resistant to “massive body” view implementations

Core Principle

SwiftUI UI is a hierarchy of views.

A parent view should describe structure.
Child views should encapsulate reusable or conceptually distinct pieces.
Containers should express intent, not just appearance.
Modifiers should refine a view rather than obscure its role.

Prefer a hierarchy that explains:

1. what the screen contains
2. how pieces relate
3. which parts are reusable
4. which parts are conditional
5. which parts are decorative vs structural

Architectural Translation

Translate Apple’s view-hierarchy model into these project rules:

- Every feature screen must have one clear root composition view.
- Large screens must be split into semantic subviews.
- Each extracted subview must have a single responsibility.
- Container choice must reflect meaning, not convenience.
- Decoration must not be allowed to distort structural hierarchy.
- Conditional UI must preserve intent and readability.
- View bodies must remain declarative composition layers, not logic dumps.

---

## HIERARCHY DESIGN

Each view should describe one level of the UI hierarchy.

A view body should answer:

- what are this view’s immediate children?
- what container relationship do they have?
- what state/configuration affects them?

Rules

- Keep each `body` focused on immediate composition.
- Extract child views when a branch has its own meaning.
- Avoid deeply nested anonymous layout code when a named subview would clarify intent.
- Prefer semantic view names over generic names.

Good

struct ClientDetailScreen: View {
var body: some View {
ClientDetailContent()
}
}

struct ClientDetailContent: View {
var body: some View {
VStack {
ClientHeaderSection()
ClientContactSection()
ClientNotesSection()
}
}
}
Bad
struct ClientDetailScreen: View {
var body: some View {
VStack {
HStack {
VStack {
Text("Name")
Text("Email")
Text("Phone")
}
}
VStack {
Text("Notes")
TextEditor(text: .constant(""))
}
}
}
}
ONE STRUCTURAL CONCERN PER VIEW
A view should own one structural concern.
Examples:

screen root
section
row
card
inspector pane
toolbar content
overlay badge
empty state
Rules
Do not mix screen orchestration, section composition, and leaf rendering in one view.
Split “screen”, “section”, and “row/item” responsibilities into separate types.
Extract leaf views before extracting layout helpers.
Prefer private helper views over large inline branches.
Preferred hierarchy
Screen
└─ Content
├─ Section
│ ├─ Row
│ └─ Row
└─ Section

Avoid

Screen
└─ one 300-line body with all sections, rows, and decorations inline

CONTAINER SELECTION
Use containers that match intent.
Choose structure based on relationship:

use stack/grid/layout containers for layout relationships
use grouping/container APIs for purpose-driven grouping
use Group when you need logical grouping without changing layout semantics
use higher-level containers when the UI is semantically a form, control group, section, or similar
Rules
Do not use a generic container when a more meaningful one exists.
Do not introduce containers solely to “make code compile” if they blur intent.
Prefer containers that explain purpose to future maintainers.
Use Group for structural grouping, conditional grouping, or modifier scoping when no layout container is intended.
Good
Group {
if model.isLoading {
LoadingView()
} else {
LoadedContentView(model: model)
}
}
Bad
VStack {
if model.isLoading {
LoadingView()
} else {
LoadedContentView(model: model)
}
}
Use a layout container only when layout is the reason it exists.
MODIFIER PLACEMENT
Modifiers configure views in the hierarchy.
They should reinforce structure, not conceal it.
Rules

Apply modifiers as close as possible to the view they affect.
Apply shared modifiers at the lowest common ancestor.
Do not stack long modifier chains on giant container views when only some descendants need them.
Prefer extracting a subview over creating an unreadable modifier chain.
Keep styling modifiers separate from structural composition when possible.
Good
ClientHeaderSection(client: client)
.padding()
.background(.thinMaterial)
Good
VStack(alignment: .leading) {
ClientHeaderSection(client: client)
ClientNotesSection(client: client)
}
.padding()
Bad
VStack {
ClientHeaderSection(client: client)
ClientNotesSection(client: client)
FooterView()
}
.padding()
.background(.thinMaterial)
.font(.headline)
.foregroundStyle(.secondary)
.lineLimit(1)
The container now obscures which modifiers are structural and which are accidental.
SUBVIEW EXTRACTION
Extract subviews when they improve:
readability
reuse
testability
previewability
ownership boundaries
Rules
Extract a subview when:

a branch has a clear domain name
a branch is reused
a branch has distinct state/configuration
a branch needs separate previews
a branch exceeds comfortable scanning size
Do not extract a subview when:
the extraction produces a meaningless name
the extraction hides important local context
the extracted view exists only to avoid scrolling
Preferred extraction thresholds
repeated UI -> always extract
named conceptual region -> extract
deeply nested conditional branch -> usually extract
tiny one-off leaf with no semantic value -> keep inline
LAYERING
Use layering intentionally.
Prefer APIs that express parent-child decoration relationships:

.overlay
.background
Use stack-based layering only when multiple sibling layers are truly peers.
Rules

Use .background for background decoration attached to a view.
Use .overlay for adornments, outlines, badges, progress masks, or attached foreground layers.
Do not default to ZStack when the layer is conceptually attached to one base view.
Keep decorative layers separate from structural child content.
Good
Text(client.name)
.padding(8)
.background(.quaternary, in: RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 8))
Good
AvatarView(client: client)
.overlay(alignment: .bottomTrailing) {
StatusDot(isOnline: client.isActive)
}
Bad
ZStack {
RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 8).fill(.quaternary)
Text(client.name)
}
Use ZStack only when the layered views are true peers in composition.
HIDING VS REMOVING
Treat “hidden” and “not in the hierarchy” as different architectural decisions.
Rules

Remove a view conditionally when it should not participate in structure.
Hide a view without removal only when you intentionally want to preserve layout role, identity, or surrounding composition behavior.
Do not leave inactive UI in the hierarchy by default.
Do not use opacity as a substitute for structural decisions.
Make conditional presence explicit in the hierarchy.
Good
if model.hasWarning {
WarningBanner(message: model.warningMessage)
}
Good
StatusIcon()
.hidden(!showsStatusIcon)
Only use this pattern when preserved layout/placement is desired.
Bad

WarningBanner(message: model.warningMessage)
.opacity(model.hasWarning ? 1 : 0)
This keeps the view structurally present without making that intention clear.
CONDITIONAL COMPOSITION
Conditional branches should preserve readability and identity.
Rules

Prefer if branches that swap meaningful subtrees.
Prefer extracting conditional branches into named subviews when they are substantial.
Keep conditional logic shallow in body.
Avoid pyramids of nested if / switch directly inside large screens.
Good
var body: some View {
Group {
switch model.phase {
case .loading:
LoadingStateView()
case .empty:
EmptyStateView()
case .loaded:
LoadedClientContent(model: model)
case .failed(let error):
ErrorStateView(error: error)
}
}
}
Bad
var body: some View {
VStack {
if model.phase == .loading {
ProgressView()
} else {
if model.phase == .empty {
Text("Empty")
} else {
if case .loaded = model.phase {
// lots of inline content
} else {
Text("Error")
}
}
}
}
}
ROOT VIEW COMPOSITION
Feature root views should compose structure, not implement every detail.
Rules

A feature root view should establish the primary container structure.
Root views may connect feature state to subviews.
Root views should delegate leaf rendering to child views.
Root views should remain readable from top to bottom.
Preferred example
struct BillingHubView: View {
@State private var selection: Invoice.ID?

    var body: some View {
        NavigationSplitView {
            BillingSidebar(selection: $selection)
        } detail: {
            BillingDetailPane(selection: selection)
        }
    }

}
Not preferred
struct BillingHubView: View {
var body: some View {
NavigationSplitView {
List { ... many rows ... }
} detail: {
ScrollView {
VStack {
// all detail rendering inline
// all overlays inline
// all empty states inline
// all toolbars inline
}
}
}
}
}
PROJECT-SCALE RULES
For a large macOS SwiftUI app:
Keep app/scene structure separate from feature composition.
Keep each feature screen split into screen/section/row layers.
Prefer dedicated files for major composed subviews.
Co-locate private helper views with their feature when reuse is local.
Promote subviews to shared modules only after true cross-feature reuse appears.
Suggested file pattern
Features/
BillingHub/
BillingHubView.swift
BillingSidebar.swift
BillingDetailPane.swift
BillingEmptyState.swift
Components/
InvoiceRow.swift
ClientChip.swift

ANTI-PATTERNS
Massive Body View
One view contains:
navigation
layout
styling
conditional states
row rendering
overlays
alerts
toolbars
Meaningless Extraction
Extracting TopPartView, MiddleThingView, Content2View
Container Abuse
Using VStack or ZStack where the code is logically grouping/configuring,
not laying out
Modifier Fog
Long modifier chains on ancestors that make descendant behavior unclear
Fake Hiding
Using .opacity(0) or off-screen tricks when the view should be conditionally removed
Decorative Structure Pollution
Using structural containers primarily to implement background/overlay decoration
Conditional Pyramids
Deeply nested inline branches instead of named state subtrees
CHECKLIST
Before finalizing a SwiftUI view hierarchy, verify:
Does this view describe one level of hierarchy clearly?
Are major branches extracted into semantic subviews?
Is each container present for a real structural reason?
Are modifiers attached at the correct level?
Are overlays/backgrounds used instead of unnecessary stack layering?
Is conditional inclusion explicit and readable?
Is hidden-vs-removed behavior intentional?
Could another developer understand the screen structure in one pass?
ENFORCEMENT SUMMARY
Always prefer:
small semantic views
explicit hierarchy
purpose-driven containers
localized modifiers
intentional layering
explicit conditional composition
Never allow:
monolithic bodies
accidental containers
decorative structure leakage
opaque modifier chains
ambiguous hiding behavior
